    "isFreeTrial": false,
    "isVip": false,
    "keyNotes": "• A line is a mark whose length dominates its width, and it does two jobs at once: it describes an edge and it carries the mood of the drawing.\n• Constructed lines are ruled with T-square and set square; they are the lines required in graphic layout, technical work and the geometry of a kente strip.\n• Expressive lines are freehand and carry feeling: a broken pressing line reads as old thatch, a long sweeping line reads as speed, a heavy trembling line reads as weight or age.\n• A contour line records the outer edge of a form; a cross-contour line runs across its surface like the lines of longitude and shows how the surface turns away from the eye.\n• Hatching is a set of parallel strokes at a consistent angle, usually near 45 degrees to the horizontal, spaced about 1 to 2 mm apart; closing the spacing darkens the tone.\n• Cross-hatching lays two or more hatch layers at angles to one another, and because each layer stays visible it is the most controllable value scale available: count layers, not pressure.\n• Stippling builds tone from small dots, density per square centimetre giving the value; it is slow, cannot smudge, and reads at any angle, so it suits technical illustration.\n• Scumbling is an open irregular circular scribble that leaves paper showing through the loops, suited to sponge, foam, netting, cloud and broken wall crust.\n• Blending rubs graphite into the tooth with tissue, fingertip or blending stump for a smooth continuous tone; it works on hot press paper and turns to patch on rough paper.\n• Tone is built light to dark in layers; a 6B laid in one pass cannot be lifted again and grooves the paper where the marker sees the scratch.\n• Confidence drills under time pressure: fill an A4 box with parallel lines at 3 mm in two minutes, then the same box converging to one marked point, then a five-step ladder.\n• Draw from the shoulder and not the wrist: a wrist pivot arcs only a few centimetres and hooks every line at its end, a shoulder pivot swings a long smooth arc.\n• To use the shoulder pivot, grip high on the barrel, lock the wrist, sight the two end points, then draw the stroke in one movement at an even speed.\n• Four line widths come from one grade: knife point, rounded edge, chisel edge and side of lead; width is a property of the tool edge, not of the pencil grade.\n• Line weight alone can create depth: a heavier foreground line against a thinner background line makes space read without any tone at all.",
