          "content": "A contour line records the outer edge of a form as it is seen; it is the silhouette and the shape line of a still-life object. A cross-contour line runs across the surface of that form like the lines of longitude on a globe, and it is the honest way to describe how a surface turns away from the eye. Draw cross-contour lines on a gourd, a calabash or a plastic water keg and the reason a straight line cannot describe a curved body becomes obvious: the contour closes while the cross-contour bends, and the spacing of the cross-contour tells the eye how fast the surface is curving. Blind contour, in which the eye never leaves the object, trains eye-hand coordination but distorts the shape, so it is kept as a warm-up; modified contour allows a glance at the paper so that proportion can be corrected while drawing continues. Cross-contour also teaches the second law of this topic: an edge nearer the eye is drawn heavier and sharper, an edge turning away is drawn lighter, so tone is already present in a drawing made of nothing but line.",
