          "title": "Mark-Making for Tone: Hatching, Cross-Hatching, Stippling, Scumbling and Blending",
          "content": "Each tonal mark is a contract with the eye, and the contract is about density. Hatching is a set of parallel strokes held at a consistent angle, commonly near 45 degrees to the horizontal and spaced 1 to 2 millimetres apart, and it darkens simply as the spacing closes. Cross-hatching adds a second and third layer at a different angle; because every layer remains visible, it is the most controllable method in the set, and a five-step value ladder can be built from one 2B pencil by counting layers rather than by pressing harder. Stippling replaces the stroke with dots, density per square centimetre being the value; it carries no visible direction, cannot smudge and reads at any angle, which makes it the correct choice for a technical illustration that will be handled and mounted. Scumbling is an open irregular circular scribble that leaves paper shining through the loops, ideal for sponge, foam, netting, cloud and the crusted surface of an old mud wall. Blending rubs graphite into the tooth with tissue, fingertip or blending stump to give a smooth continuous tone; it performs well on hot press paper and fails on rough paper, where rubbing only pushes lead into the hollows and produces a patch. A serious drawing combines at least three of these marks and keeps each one legible.",
