          "title": "Letterform families and anatomy",
          "content": "Before drawing letters you must be able to talk about them. The baseline is the ground line every letter stands on; cap height is the distance from baseline to the top of an uppercase letter; x-height is the height of a lowercase vowel, and letters like b and p break above and below it with ascenders and descenders. The stem is the main upright stroke, the bowl is a curved closed stroke as in b and o, the counter is the air trapped inside it, and a terminal is where a stroke simply ends. Serifs are the small finishing strokes at the ends of main stems; where they join with a curve they are bracketed, and where they meet at a sharp point they are called spurs. A serif face such as Times reads traditional, formal and bookish, which suits certificates and funeral programmes; a sans-serif face such as Arial reads modern, blunt and loud at distance, which is why road plates and shop boards prefer it. Commercial sign work in Ghana is overwhelmingly uppercase sans-serif monoline, so that is the alphabet you must first perfect.",
