          "content": "Good lettering begins as geometry. Fix the sheet with low-tack masking tape, rule a baseline and cap line across in hard pencil, and check the pair with set squares so the lines are truly parallel; every later error multiplies from a crooked guide line. Divide the width for the word before drawing a single letter, giving round letters like O, C and G a fraction more air than flat-sided ones like E, H and T, since identical ruler gaps make the rounds look further apart. Construct each capital in pencil first: O and C as true ellipses within a light enclosing rectangle, E with its middle arm slightly above the mathematical centre because the human eye corrects anything low to the ground, diagonals in A, V and N meeting cleanly at the apex with no spike or flat. Ink last, with hard edges traced along the construction lines, then erase all guides once dry. The whole alphabet sorts into five construction families, O and C, E and its turnings, the diagonals of A and V, the verticals of I and H, and the hybrids like N and Z; mastering one member of each family carries you through the rest.",
