          "title": "Decoding the Question Paper",
          "content": "The practical paper is a contract, and the first job is reading it like one. Circle the instruction verb, because Design, Draw, Illustrate and Plan demand different evidence: a Design question wants the scheme and thumbnails seen, a Draw question weights final execution. Underline the subject matter and box every constraint: board or sheet size, permitted media, the audience or occasion named in the passage, and how many studies must be shown. Then write the requirements as a numbered tick-list in the margin; every later decision is tested against that list, and at the end you score your own project on it. Read the whole paper before starting anything, because a later clause often changes the earlier one, for example a note that colour must be limited to three hues. Keep the annotated paper pinned in view all sitting: candidates who hand in work that answers a question they remembered instead of the question they were given lose relevance marks no amount of skill can recover.",
